<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miguel Cabello de Balboa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1535–<a href="/years/1608">1608</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miguel Cabello de Balboa was a Spanish priest and chronicler who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. His Miscelánea antártica, completed in 1586, includes a history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rulers with dates for their reigns, which modern scholars, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-howland-rowe">John Rowe</a>, used to build the standard chronology of Inca expansion.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cabello de Balboa was a great-nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-nunez-de-balboa">Vasco Núñez de Balboa</a>, who in 1513 led the first European expedition from the New World to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. He was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archidona">Archidona</a>, near Málaga; the exact date is not known. As a young man he chose a military career and fought in the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy">Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamoral-count-of-egmont">Lamoral, Count of Egmont</a>, and with Rodrigo de Basan. He was among the victors of the Battle of Gravelines in 1558 over the French army of Marshal Paul de Thermes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in Málaga in 1565 or 1566, he became a churchman and in 1566 sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. From there he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, where he began the Miscelánea antártica, and he later lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, where he also wrote verse and prose, apparently including two comedies. A letter of 1602–1603 gives detailed information on the regions of Pelechuco and Apolobamba in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, between the Andes and the Beni River, in terms that suggest he had been there.</p><p>He is also an important source on the northern Andes, especially the Pacific shore and the forests reaching inland to the cordilleras of present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, and his writings on the lowland peoples were gathered in 1945 as Verdadera descripción y relación de la provincia y tierra de las Esmeraldas. He died in 1608 at Camata, in Larecaja Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Besides its long account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> past as tradition told it, which gives their origin somewhat differently from other Spanish authors, the Miscelánea antártica records alleged Amerindian traditions of white men who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and preached the gospel, and puts forward the theory that the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> of Macassar. In 1853 the original was owned by the historiographer Joaquin Garcia Ycazbalceta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and a complete copy is held by the New York Public Library. San Marcos University published the whole work in 1951. Several of his other writings, among them La Volcánea and La Comedia del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>, are lost.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miguel Cabello de Balboa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This date comes from John Rowe’s reading of the Spanish chronicler <b>Miguel Cabello de Balboa</b>; radiocarbon dates from imperial sites suggest that Inca expansion may have begun several decades earlier, so the familiar chronology is best treated as approximate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archidona">Archidona</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.1,-4.39&amp;year=1582" aria-label="Archidona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4440 828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miguel Cabello de Balboa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4890 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4890 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.1,-4.39&amp;year=1582">What was here in 1582? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
